## The idea

The list of words does not change between queries, so do the work **once**: store every word's positions in a dictionary word → sorted list of indices. A query merges two sorted lists with **two pointers**, always advancing the smaller index, because only that side can get closer to the other. For the same word twice, the answer is the smallest gap between neighbouring positions in its own list.

## Step by step

1. Constructor: for each index i, append i to positions[words[i]] (lists come out sorted).
2. Shortest(w1, w2): missing word → KeyNotFoundException.
3. If w1 == w2: need at least two positions; return the smallest difference between neighbours.
4. Otherwise: i = 0, j = 0. While both lists have items: record |a[i] − b[j]|, then advance whichever index is smaller.

## Worked example

**Input:** ["practice","makes","perfect","coding","makes"]; Shortest("makes","coding")

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| positions | makes → [1, 4]; coding → [3] |
| a[0]=1, b[0]=3 | distance 2; 1 < 3 → advance makes |
| a[1]=4, b[0]=3 | distance 1 → best 1; 3 < 4 → advance coding → done |

**Result:** 1

## Cost

Constructor: O(n). Each query: O(a + b), the number of occurrences of the two words. Memory O(n).

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
public class WordDistance {
    private readonly Dictionary<string, List<int>> _positions = new Dictionary<string, List<int>>();

    public WordDistance(string[] words) {
        for (int i = 0; i < words.Length; i++) {
            if (!_positions.ContainsKey(words[i])) _positions[words[i]] = new List<int>();
            _positions[words[i]].Add(i);                    // indices are added in order: already sorted
        }
    }

    public int Shortest(string w1, string w2) {
        if (!_positions.ContainsKey(w1) || !_positions.ContainsKey(w2)) throw new KeyNotFoundException();
        List<int> a = _positions[w1];
        List<int> b = _positions[w2];

        if (w1 == w2) {                                     // the same word: closest two occurrences
            if (a.Count < 2) throw new ArgumentException("The word occurs only once.");
            int smallestGap = int.MaxValue;
            for (int k = 1; k < a.Count; k++) {
                if (a[k] - a[k - 1] < smallestGap) smallestGap = a[k] - a[k - 1];
            }
            return smallestGap;
        }

        int i = 0, j = 0, best = int.MaxValue;
        while (i < a.Count && j < b.Count) {
            int distance = Math.Abs(a[i] - b[j]);
            if (distance < best) best = distance;
            if (a[i] < b[j]) i++;                           // move the smaller one forward
            else j++;
        }
        return best;
    }
}
```

